            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>During the walkthrough, we found leakage on the following items:

-Receptacles

-Some windows

-Front door

-From the crawl space 

-Furnace chimney

-Wood fireplace	

During the infiltration test walkthrough, we notice air leakage on the items listed above. Airtightness is very important if you are changing your heating system. We recommend sealing the building before changing the furnace. During the infiltration test, we felt some air leakage coming from the crawl space. 

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The exterior walls insulation is estimated to be R-19 which is below the actual standard of R-28. The incentive for the increase in insulation is offered. The amount is $2.50/square foot for an addition of a minimum of R-20 and $3.50/square foot for the addition of R-30 on top of the existing R-value.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>At the time of the audit, the attic insulation inspection was not possible. We estimated the insulation level according to the year of construction. However, we recommend the addition of insulation to meet the city of Whitehorse standard of R-60. We recommend the same thing for the cathedral ceiling, but in this case, we suggest adding as much as possible according to the space available.</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors>The floor above the garage is considered as an exposed floor and could represent a big portion of your heat loss. A solution to reduce the energy loss would be to increase the insulation and seal the floor to reduce air leakage. </exposedFloors>
                <foundation>At the time of the assessment the concrete basement wall was not insulated. We recommend insulation to reduce heat loss. </foundation>
                <windows>At the time of the assessment we notice a mix of double and triple-pane windows. We recommend the replacement of the wood double panes windows by triple-pane and Energy-star certified units. </windows>
                <HeatingSystem>Changing the heating system is a big decision and it?????????s not easy to choose. To help you decide we suggest the following points:

-Source of energy 

-Efficiency

-Maintenance

-Cost (To acquire the system and maintenance)

-Your comfort 

Since you have some time ahead, we suggest taking your time to choose the best system that will meet your needs and expectations. 



The suggested upgrade is electric baseboards providing heat for the entire house. If you choose to go with electric heat, we recommend the replacement of the thermostat by programmable ones, they will give better control of your heating choice and help you save some energy.  



Have you already thought about having a heat pump. This type of heating system can use up to 3 times less energy than a typical baseboard and needs electricity to operate. However this kind of system can?????????t operate on temperature below -250C, it then will require a secondary system to maintain the heat. In this case, the furnace can ensure the heat and the ducting system can be used by the heat pump to supply heat in the entire building. You might be eligible to participate in the Heat Pumps pilot project, please follow the link below for more information;

https://yukon.ca/en/news/expanded-rebates-renewable-home-heating-now-available 



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>Concerning your electrical usage, I?????????ve listed some solutions/problems that might be useful to help you find what seems to be causing the problem. The Following items might explain and help you to investigate :



1-APPLIANCES/DEVICES: It is possible in some case that one of the appliances or devices use more energy than expected. 

SOLUTION: Turn off your electrical panel and turn on one by one the breakers one by one and take a look at your electric meter to see if you notice a higher usage. This option might help you to aim at which one seems to drain Energy.

Proceed with the same strategy with the other brokers to verify if the consumption comes from another circuit.



2-ELECTRICAL WIRES: In some cases, the wires come in contact with the electrical box (switch or plugs). In this case, the current will flow continuously, which can explain the higher usage. This situation can be caused by a natural movement of the house that has for effect to pull on the wires at the point of exposing the wires at the point of touching the metal box. 



3-ELECTRICAL USAGE MONITOR: Another option is to use an electricity usage monitor to verify the electrical usage of your appliances/devices and verify if they are similar, close, or higher than the specified by the manufacturer. 



If the electrical meter runs faster than normal when you turn on the breaker of the appliances/devices or circuit, it might be an indicator. This procedure might help you to find what is causing the increase in your consumption and select the one that seems to be wrong.



I suggest starting with these suggestions and see if you can find something, hopefully, it will help you find the problem. The report gives you an estimated electrical usage, which you can compare with your electrical usage on your ATCO bills.

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
